WEBVTT
NOTE 06-why-some-deep-sea-creatures-are-transparent - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.392
Transparency does not make an animal
disappear. It makes the edges harder to

00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:10.561
In open water, where there is no rock,
plant, or burrow to hide behind, losing an

00:00:10.561 --> 00:00:14.773
outline can be the difference between
being seen and being missed.

00:00:14.773 --> 00:00:17.264
Why some deep sea creatures are
transparent?

00:00:17.264 --> 00:00:19.751
Transparency is not magic invisibility.

00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:24.981
Eyes, digestive organs, pigments, and
reflective tissues remain difficult to

00:00:24.981 --> 00:00:29.499
The adaptation works by reducing the
visible outline enough to improve

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:34.692
The rest of this video follows that
question through the actual environmental

00:00:34.692 --> 00:00:40.087
constraints described by NOAA, MBARI,
Smithsonian, and Woods Hole sources in the

00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:41.301
companion article.

00:00:41.301 --> 00:00:46.233
The barreleye is the image I keep coming
back to because the transparent part is

00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:48.329
not a science-fiction glass skull.

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.336
It is a fragile, fluid-filled shield
around eyes that can rotate.

00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:56.759
Midwater animals cannot shelter behind
rocks, plants or the seafloor.

00:00:56.759 --> 00:01:01.823
A visible outline against downwelling
light can reveal them to a predator below

00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:02.720
or prey above.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.933
Clear, watery tissue allows more light to
pass through instead of reflecting a

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.136
strong silhouette.

00:01:09.136 --> 00:01:14.417
Gelatinous animals can be almost entirely
clear because their bodies contain large

00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:18.539
amounts of water and relatively little
light-absorbing material.

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:23.092
Muscles, eyes, digestive organs and
pigments are harder to hide.

00:01:23.092 --> 00:01:28.854
Some species conceal those opaque parts
with mirrors, red pigment, compact organs

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:30.491
or special positioning.

00:01:30.491 --> 00:01:35.328
The barreleye fish has a transparent,
fluid-filled shield over its tubular eyes.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:40.587
MBARI observations showed that the eyes
can rotate as the fish changes from

00:01:40.587 --> 00:01:42.971
looking upward to feeding forward.

00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:47.138
The shield protects the eyes while
preserving a clear field of view around

00:01:47.138 --> 00:01:48.715
stinging gelatinous animals.

00:01:48.715 --> 00:01:53.062
At greater depths where sunlight is
absent, being clear may offer less

00:01:53.062 --> 00:01:55.173
advantage than being red or black.

00:01:55.173 --> 00:02:02.325
Bioluminescent flashes can also reveal
internal organs if they are not shielded.

00:02:02.325 --> 00:02:06.829
That is why transparency often appears
alongside other strategies rather than

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:07.707
replacing them.

00:02:07.707 --> 00:02:12.324
A common question is: Are transparent
animals invisible? No.

00:02:12.324 --> 00:02:17.557
Edges, eyes, organs and changes in
refraction can still reveal them.

00:02:17.557 --> 00:02:21.455
A common question is: Why are many jellies
transparent?

00:02:21.455 --> 00:02:26.983
Their water-rich tissues transmit light
and reduce a strong silhouette in open

00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:27.408
water.

00:02:27.408 --> 00:02:31.833
A common question is: Is the barreleye's
entire head empty? No.

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:37.382
A transparent fluid-filled shield covers
and protects its eyes; the rest of the

00:02:37.382 --> 00:02:40.192
head contains normal tissues and organs.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:44.411
A common question is: Do transparent
animals live only in the deep sea?

00:02:44.411 --> 00:02:50.037
No. Transparency also occurs in plankton
and other animals closer to the surface.

00:02:50.037 --> 00:02:52.414
A body is not one optical material.

00:02:52.414 --> 00:02:57.845
Gelatinous tissue can transmit light well,
but pigment, food, eyes, muscles, and

00:02:57.845 --> 00:03:00.221
internal organs are harder to hide.

00:03:00.221 --> 00:03:05.336
That is why many transparent animals still
have visible guts or eyes.

00:03:05.336 --> 00:03:10.440
Some solve the problem with mirrors,
pigment, narrow body shapes, or

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.721
The barreleye is an important correction:
its shield is transparent, but the whole

00:03:15.721 --> 00:03:16.493
fish is not.

00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:21.826
MBARI's live ROV observations showed that
the tubular eyes rotate inside that

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:26.259
shield, a feature that preserved specimens
had failed to reveal.

00:03:26.259 --> 00:03:31.139
Transparency sounds like a perfect
solution until the body has to digest

00:03:31.139 --> 00:03:36.291
food, move muscles, carry pigments,
protect a nervous system, and reproduce.

00:03:36.291 --> 00:03:38.911
Those structures interact with light.

00:03:38.911 --> 00:03:43.656
A transparent animal can still reveal its
gut, eyes, eggs, or prey.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:48.729
That is why open-water camouflage often
combines partial transparency with

00:03:48.729 --> 00:03:54.488
reflective surfaces, small size, vertical
posture, counterillumination, or behavior.

00:03:54.488 --> 00:03:58.030
The goal is not literal invisibility from
every angle.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:03.080
It is reducing contrast against the
background seen by a particular predator.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.130
The barreleye makes the distinction
especially clear.

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:09.699
Its transparent shield does not make the
whole fish disappear.

00:04:09.699 --> 00:04:14.311
It lets the tubular eyes look upward
through protected tissue and rotate as the

00:04:14.311 --> 00:04:16.413
animal changes its feeding position.

00:04:16.413 --> 00:04:21.380
Net-caught specimens once obscured that
anatomy because the shield was damaged.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.461
Live ROV observations changed the
interpretation.

00:04:24.461 --> 00:04:30.004
That history is a useful reminder for the
entire channel: specimen photographs and

00:04:30.004 --> 00:04:32.776
live behavior answer different questions.

00:04:32.776 --> 00:04:36.189
A specimen can support anatomy and
identification.

00:04:36.189 --> 00:04:41.717
A live observation can reveal posture,
movement, orientation, and the function of

00:04:41.717 --> 00:04:44.379
structures that collapse after capture.

00:04:44.379 --> 00:04:48.907
The humanized version of this episode
should let the audience feel that

00:04:48.907 --> 00:04:53.755
The strange feature is not simply
displayed; the evidence changes the

00:04:53.755 --> 00:04:58.200
The most convincing transparent animals
are never uniformly invisible.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:03.533
Their digestive systems, eyes,
reproductive tissue, or recent meals can

00:05:03.533 --> 00:05:05.550
That imperfection is the story.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.779
Evolution does not get to remove every
organ that scatters light.

00:05:09.779 --> 00:05:13.032
It works around the organs the animal
still needs.

00:05:13.032 --> 00:05:19.611
Some species narrow or reposition opaque
structures. Some use mirrors or pigments.

00:05:19.611 --> 00:05:24.373
Some stay small. Some orient their bodies
to present less visible area.

00:05:24.373 --> 00:05:29.043
The video should pause on an animal whose
gut remains visible and explain that

00:05:29.043 --> 00:05:32.635
transparency is a reduction in detection,
not a magic cloak.

00:05:32.635 --> 00:05:37.483
This makes the adaptation feel more
biological and less like a visual effect.

00:05:37.483 --> 00:05:42.602
It also prevents us from claiming that a
transparent creature disappears equally

00:05:42.602 --> 00:05:46.057
well to every predator, wavelength, and
viewing angle.

00:05:46.057 --> 00:05:49.704
The animal is playing probabilities, not
becoming absent.

00:05:49.704 --> 00:05:54.261
Camouflage is measured against another
animal's visual system, not a human

00:05:54.261 --> 00:05:56.355
watching a color-corrected screen.

00:05:56.355 --> 00:06:01.258
Predators may detect contrast,
polarization, motion, or wavelengths our

00:06:01.258 --> 00:06:03.123
display does not reproduce.

00:06:03.123 --> 00:06:08.237
A body that looks obvious in a paused
frame may be harder to detect while

00:06:08.237 --> 00:06:12.801
The script should therefore say 'reduces
visibility' rather than 'becomes

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:17.677
invisible.' That wording is both more
accurate and more interesting because it

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:19.677
keeps the predator in the story.

00:06:19.677 --> 00:06:25.592
Place a transparent animal over three
backgrounds: open blue water, black water

00:06:25.592 --> 00:06:29.336
under ROV light, and a high-contrast
graphic grid.

00:06:29.336 --> 00:06:33.136
The animal's visibility changes each time.
That is the point.

00:06:33.136 --> 00:06:38.120
Transparency is contextual, and a camera
designed to expose faint structures can

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.861
make successful camouflage look
ineffective.

00:06:40.861 --> 00:06:44.992
The final edit should celebrate the
structures revealed by the camera while

00:06:44.992 --> 00:06:48.792
explaining that a predator does not
receive the same optimized image.

00:06:48.792 --> 00:06:53.645
The difference between our sensor and the
predator's eye belongs in the explanation,

00:06:53.645 --> 00:06:54.685
not in a footnote.

00:06:54.685 --> 00:06:59.354
The difference between our sensor and the
predator's eye belongs in the explanation,

00:06:59.354 --> 00:07:00.355
not in a footnote.

00:07:00.355 --> 00:07:03.758
Use an anatomy case study rather than a
general list.

00:07:03.758 --> 00:07:08.382
Begin with a silhouette, reveal the
transparent tissue, then isolate the

00:07:08.382 --> 00:07:11.336
structures that still scatter or absorb
light.

00:07:11.336 --> 00:07:16.145
For the barreleye, rely on an original
diagram unless a licensed clip is

00:07:16.145 --> 00:07:21.421
admitted; MBARI's reporting supports the
facts, but the site's copyright notice

00:07:21.421 --> 00:07:26.296
means its images and footage are not
automatically commercial-use assets.

00:07:26.296 --> 00:07:29.030
Transparency is not perfect invisibility.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:34.632
It works best in open water and against
certain viewing angles and light

00:07:34.632 --> 00:07:38.275
It can be costly because tissues still
need structure and protection.

00:07:38.275 --> 00:07:42.551
The barreleye's transparent shield should
not be generalized to all barreleyes or

00:07:42.551 --> 00:07:45.613
all transparent animals without
species-specific evidence.

00:07:45.613 --> 00:07:50.320
MBARI's feeding scenario around
siphonophores is presented as a working

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.171
hypothesis, not direct proof of every
meal.

00:07:53.171 --> 00:07:56.200
The deep ocean does not need an
invisibility cloak.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.536
Sometimes it only needs a body that gives
the predator less to lock onto.

00:08:00.536 --> 00:08:02.656
Transparency is not nothing.

00:08:02.656 --> 00:08:08.637
It is a carefully managed compromise
between being alive and being hard to see.
